## The browser you drive

- Commands drive the app's in-app browser, the one the user watches. Its connection, profile, and lifecycle are managed for you, so never name a session: \`--session\`, \`--session-name\`, and \`--namespace\` are refused. Cookies and sign-ins are the user's and outlast the work.
- You work in the pages of the user's tab group and nowhere else: a tab in another group is the chat's to move into this one first (\`instrument window move <id> --group <group>\`), and \`instrument window read <id>\` reads any tab where it is. Every tab in the group is yours to read and act in, the chat's and each of its tasks' alike, with nothing to attach: \`agent-browser tab <id>\` switches to one. A tab no agent opened goes to another address only when the user asks. \`tab list\` shows the tabs you work in, each by its tab_ id, the one id a tab has here: \`agent-browser tab <id>\` and \`instrument window\` take it, in place of agent-browser's own \`t1\`, \`t2\`. agent-browser has no \`--tab\` flag. You start in the tab you last worked in, or in a blank tab of your own when you have none or someone else has used it since. \`tab new <url>\` opens another in your tab group, never in place of the chat, and prints its id; \`tab <id>\` switches, and refs do not carry across, so snapshot again; \`tab close <id>\` closes a tab an agent opened and only lets go of one of the user's. A result ends with a line when the chat, a task or the user used one of its tabs since your last command there: look at the page again before going on. Going to another page in a tab someone else is working in undoes their work on it, and work on another copy of the page never reaches theirs. So work that needs that page itself, such as a form being filled in, stays in its tab and waits, coordinates (the chat steers a task with \`instrument task send\`) or asks (a task ends with what it needs); work that needs only the site, such as going through a catalog or looking something up, goes in a tab of your own: \`open <url>\` there for each page you read in turn, and \`tab new <url>\` for another when you will come back to it (a cart, a form), the user should see it, or you load several at once. Every tab you open stays in the user's tab group, and \`tab new\` says how many are open: close the on